
PART A: 2048 GAME
The 2048 game is split into two files to keep the code organized: main.cpp runs
the game menu and player turns, while 2048_helper.h handles the board movements
and file saving. Instead of writing separate movement code for Up, Down, Left,
and Right, all four directions are simplified into single lines that are solved
by one function: process_line(). This function slides numbers together, merges
matching neighbor tiles to add to the score, and slides them once more to close
any empty spaces. A new random '2' tile only appears if the board actually
moved. When saving or loading from savegame.txt, the program carefully checks
that the saved scores and moves make sense and that every tile on the board is
either a zero or a proper power of two.

PART B: LUHN CHECKSUM VALIDATOR
The Luhn program checks if a 16-digit credit card number is formatted correctly
and mathematically valid. It reads the card number as text so leading zeros
are not lost, and it immediately rejects any input that has spaces, letters,
or is not exactly 16 numbers long. If the format is correct, the checkLuhn()
function reads the number backwards from right to left. It leaves the first
number alone, doubles every second number (subtracting 9 if the result is 10 or
higher), and adds all the numbers together. If the final total can be divided
evenly by 10, the card number passes. The program stays open in a loop so the
user can test as many numbers as they want or type 'E' to exit.

Design choices:
The core sliding and merging engine (process_line) handles only movement without any random behavior, leaving new tiles to be spawned separately in main.cpp only when a move actually changes the board. Instead of writing separate sliding and merging code for every direction, all four moves (move_up, move_down, move_left, move_right) break down into single rows or columns that are handled identically by process_line. Saving writes the score, move count, and board numbers to savegame.txt, while loading strictly checks that moves and scores aren't negative and every tile is either zero or a power of two.

Key functions:
The game's tile mechanics rely on three key functions: process_line updates a single line in three steps by sliding tiles forward, merging identical adjacent pairs and adding to the score, and sliding them forward again to close any new gaps (returning true only if the line changed). When a valid move occurs, insert_random searches the board for empty spaces marked 0, randomly selects one, and places a 2 inside it. Finally, game_over checks if the player can still continue, immediately returning false if any open zeros remain or if adjacent matching tiles can still be merged.

